    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5194/acp-12-9479-2012",
    "abstract": "Abstract. We examine the formation of nitrate and ammonium on five types of externally mixed pre-existing aerosols using the hybrid dynamic method in a global chemistry transport model. The model developed here predicts a similar spatial pattern of total aerosol nitrate and ammonium to that of several pioneering studies, but separates the effects of nitrate and ammonium on pure sulfate, biomass burning, fossil fuel, dust and sea salt aerosols. Nitrate and ammonium boost the scattering efficiency of sulfate and organic matter but lower the extinction of sea salt particles since the hygroscopicity of a mixed nitrate-ammonium-sea salt particle is less than that of pure sea salt. The direct anthropogenic forcing of particulate nitrate and ammonium at the top of the atmosphere (TOA) is estimated to be \u22120.12 W m\u22122. Nitrate, ammonium and nitric acid gas also affect aerosol activation and the reflectivity of clouds. The first aerosol indirect forcing by anthropogenic nitrate (gas plus aerosol) and ammonium is estimated to be \u22120.09 W m\u22122 at the TOA, almost all of which is due to condensation of nitric acid gas onto growing droplets (\u22120.08 W m\u22122).",
    "field": "Aerosol-climate interactions",
    "problem": "Existing global aerosol models inadequately represent nitrate and ammonium aerosols by often excluding them, using oversimplified equilibrium assumptions that misrepresent their size distribution, and completely neglecting their indirect radiative forcing, which creates uncertainty in climate simulations.",
    "method": "This study uses a global chemistry transport model with the hybrid dynamic method to simulate nitrate and ammonium formation on five types of externally mixed aerosols, allowing for the quantification of their separate direct and indirect radiative effects.",
    "task": "The task is to simulate the global formation of nitrate and ammonium aerosols and to quantify their direct and indirect radiative effects using a global chemistry transport model.",
